<p>Thanks for the feedback! And good catch!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Long&nbsp;answer</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>As you figured out, those numbers were not random, they were all based on <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E2%84%AF">e</a>. Of course, if we assume that the aliens use a base-e counting system as opposed to our base-10 counting system (or something else entirely), we end up with the Aliens using meters as opposed to some truly Alien measurement. I could have gotten rid of the meters and just made everything mathematical gibberish, but then no would _ever_ have figured out that the aliens think in base-e as opposed to base-10, which I personally think is too intriguing of a concept to ignore.</p>
<p>As for the Earth being insignificant&#8230; I know. Believe me, I know. I've read the Xeelee Sequence. I am well aware that the Earth orbits one tiny insignificant Star out of a trillion in our Milky Way, which in turn is one galaxy out of a Trillion in our Universe. We are truly tiny. Compared to our galaxy, we are but a single skin cell is to a human&#8230; or an ant is to the United Nations.</p>
<p>But even a colony of ants can have disastrous results if they're not exterminated or quarantined properly. Imagine a colony of ants chewing through some Cold War era computer somewhere, and some nukes accidentally get set off, and that causes a chain reaction that results in nuclear war for humanity. That's what happened here.</p>
<p>And to be honest, they didn't really harm galaxy or even a good portion of it. There were only 271 or casualties total&#8230; there are still a few trillion stars in the Galaxy to keep things going. Not to mention all the other galaxies.</p>
